It doesn't seem to be a common complaint, but OS X users who have
enjoyed the tty version of Nethack in other *nixes and Windows with
the IBMgraphics option on feel a little disappointment in playing the
tty version of the game with the traditional characterset. It's funny
that it should matter so much, but the immersion level is much greater
when the walls are continuous and the hallways look dim.

So that was the reason I had not played Nethack after I switched to OS
X last year (aside from enjoying my productivity). Well, enter
GLterm.

http://www.pollet.net/GLterm/

This program actually uses OpenGL to draw fonts on the screen. What
fonts? standard .bdf fonts! GLterm doesn't hook into the OS X font
framework, but rather uses your GPU to draw *nix bdf fonts. I use
vga11x19.bdf, found here for instance:

http://dir.filewatcher.com/d/SuSE/1/pub/vga11x19.tar.gz.11708.html

This is a very large vga font suitable for Nethack with IBMgraphics
on. Now, to be fair, this isn't the first post to mention GLterm, but
the only other one wrote it off as nagware, which it is. I don't find
it particularly nagging myself, and you could always pay the $10 that
he's asking for it (I  myself buy shareware when it is excellent --
Textmate and SuperDuper! for instance).

Of course, this program makes reality the BBspot article linked on the
Nethack home page: "New ATI Card Pushes Limits in ASCII Gaming". 1000
FPS indeed! Wait till I sick a x1900xt on it! Maybe I'll even be able
to get native resolution! (btw, I thought this was amusing enough to
tell my geek leaning friends, but by the time I was done trying to
explain what Nethack even was, the irony had kind of lost its funny.
So not a recommended anecdote)

Well, thought I'd share with the other OS X nethack devotees.



